# data/nexcess-known-hosts
#
# Pinned SSH host keys for the Nexcess estate. Collected 2026-08-25 with
# `ssh-keyscan -t rsa,ecdsa,ed25519` against every host in the control-plane
# scan, 22 of 22.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, and why it is not `StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new`
# in CI:
#
# Nexcess issues no read-only SSH user. The key this scan carries can WRITE to
# every client site it reaches. `accept-new` trusts whatever answers on first
# contact, which on a laptop is a reasonable trade and on an unattended runner
# is handing a write-capable credential to a stranger. Pinning means the runner
# refuses a host it does not recognise.
#
# It is a control, not a config convenience. Same category as the command list.
#
# VERIFIED, not merely collected: the ed25519 key for 61ae96832c.nxcli.net in
# this file is SHA256:5NOYB900r/J12Q5EnnLblAXGHPBEVfd2jbKWQOtRzIM, which is
# byte-identical to the fingerprint shown at the interactive prompt on first
# connection and to the entry it wrote into ~/.ssh/known_hosts. Three sources,
# one answer.
#
# WHEN A KEY CHANGES the scan will FAIL for that host, loudly, and that is the
# intended behaviour. Do not regenerate this file to make a failure go away.
# Find out why the key changed first: a Nexcess rebuild and an interception
# look identical from here, and only one of them is fine.
#
# TO REGENERATE, deliberately, after establishing why:
#   ./scripts/nexcess-ssh-targets.py --history ./history --format tsv \
#     | awk '{print $3}' | sort -u > /tmp/h.txt
#   ssh-keyscan -t rsa,ecdsa,ed25519 -f /tmp/h.txt | grep -v '^#' | sort -u \
#     > data/nexcess-known-hosts
#
